This is a classic "Hidden Seconds Mismatch" bug that causes terrible User Experience in date/time pickers.
### Why this is happening
When you wrote this line:
minTime={dayjs().add(30, 'minute')}
You told the TimePicker that the minimum time is exactly 30 minutes from *right now, down to the exact millisecond*.
For example, if it is 10:00:45 AM, the minTime is evaluated as 10:30:45 AM.
However, when a user clicks 10:30 on the UI clock dial, the picker sets the selected time to 10:30:00 AM (zero seconds). Because 10:30:00 is technically *before* 10:30:45, MUI immediately flags it as invalid and throws the red error, even though the user clicked what looked like a valid minute!
### The Fix
You need to force dayjs to strip the seconds and milliseconds off the minTime calculation so it perfectly matches the zero-second time the user selects from the clock UI.
Scroll down to **STEP 3** in your code (around line 950) and update your <TimePicker> component to include .startOf('minute'):
```javascript
              {/* SMOOTH COLLAPSE FOR TIMEPICKER */}
              <Collapse in={routingChoice === "SCHEDULE"}>
                <Box sx={{ p: 3, mb: 4, border: '1px solid', borderColor: 'divider', borderRadius: 2 }}>
                  <Typography variant="subtitle2" fontWeight="bold" align="left" gutterBottom>
                    Select Time
                  </Typography>
                  <LocalizationProvider dateAdapter={AdapterDayjs}>
                    <TimePicker
                      value={scheduledTime}
                      onChange={(newValue) => setScheduledTime(newValue)}
                      
                      // --- THE FIX IS HERE ---
                      // .startOf('minute') forces seconds to :00 so it perfectly matches the UI selection
                      minTime={dayjs().add(30, 'minute').startOf('minute')} 
                      
                      maxTime={dayjs().endOf('day')}
                      timeSteps={{ minutes: 1 }}
                      slotProps={{
                        textField: {
                          size: 'medium',
                          fullWidth: true,
                          sx: { caretColor: 'transparent' },
                          helperText: "Execution must be scheduled at least 30 minutes from now, before 11:59 PM today.", 
                          onKeyDown: (e) => e.preventDefault(),
                          onPaste: (e) => e.preventDefault(),
                          inputProps: { readOnly: true }
                        }
                      }}
                    />
                  </LocalizationProvider>
                </Box>
              </Collapse>

```
### Why does it still show the grayed-out minutes?
MUI DatePickers will physically render the invalid numbers (like 10:28, 10:29) but gray them out. It does this because removing numbers dynamically from a clock face or a standardized dropdown list causes the UI to jump around unpredictably, which is considered bad accessibility design.
By adding .startOf('minute'), those past minutes will remain properly disabled, but the exact 30-minute mark (e.g., 10:30) will now be 100% valid and will never trigger that frustrating red outline when clicked!
